    "reading": {
        "body": "Colorado passed conversational AI operator requirements and automated decision-making rules in 2026, but failed every data center and utility modernization bill it considered.\n\nThe pattern is a legislature regulating the outputs of AI while declining to govern its physical inputs. HB1263, on conversational AI service operators, and SB189, on automated decision-making technology, both passed. SB102 on large-load data centers, HB1030 on data center and utility modernization, and HB1246 on consumer-regulated electric utilities all failed. The same session also passed SB051 on age attestation for computing devices and HB1195 on psychotherapy AI restrictions, and vetoed HB1210 on surveillance price and wage setting.\n\nFor AI specifically, this matters because the compute layer is where the grid constraint binds. A state can write operator duties for chatbots and still leave the power and siting questions for large loads unresolved. The failed bills are the ones that would have touched interconnection, utility cost allocation, and data center load directly.\n\nWhat this does not show is why the data center bills failed, whether they return, or how the passed AI measures are enforced. It covers one session's bill list and two news items, not litigation, agency rules, or federal action. The count is 32 bills, and the record does not say how many were introduced in total.",
